   "text": "I read the molecule in three parts and checked each against the drawing.\n\n- **Left end:** a 10H-phenothiazine with a nitro group on one benzo ring and an ether oxygen on the other. Both substituents sit meta to the ring sulfur.\n- **Linker:** the aryl ether oxygen is attached to a cyclobutane at C1, and C3 of the ring carries a second ether oxygen. That oxygen leads to a three-carbon chain (O\u2013CH2CH2CH2) running to a piperazine nitrogen. The piperazine's other nitrogen is bonded to the aromatic ring of the isoindoline-1,3-dione (phthalimide).\n- **Right end:** the phthalimide nitrogen carries a 2,6-dioxopiperidin-3-yl group, drawn with no stereo marking at C3, so I left it unspecified.\n\nFor the cyclobutane, the aryl-O bond on C1 is drawn as a wedge and the O bond on C3 as a hash. Opposite faces means a trans-1,3 arrangement. A 1,3-disubstituted cyclobutane has no true chiral centres, so only the relative (trans) geometry matters. I worked out the @/@@ labels for the SMILES atom order so that the two oxygens come out trans.\n\nSMILES: O=C1NC(=O)CCC1N1C(=O)c2ccc(cc2C1=O)N1CCN(CC1)CCCO[C@H]1C[C@@H](C1)Oc1ccc2Nc3ccc(cc3Sc2c1)[N+](=O)[O-]"
